## E. Reviewer output rule

1. Read this file first. Do not re-flag anything in B; if you think a B item is broken, say so with the exact failing input.
2. Report only issues you can exploit or trigger: name the attacker, the request or input, and the resulting harm. No defense-in-depth lists, no style, no theoretical hardening.
3. Each finding: `[SEVERITY] [confidence] file:line, one-line title`, then attacker, steps, impact, fix. Severity is HIGH (money, other users' data, account takeover, RCE/SSRF), MEDIUM (cross-user availability, quota or limit bypass, PII leak), LOW (self-only). Confidence is high, medium or low; say what you could not verify.
4. Check section D and the open observations before reporting; a match is "already known", not a finding.
5. Skip fast: if every changed file is CSS, markdown, copy-only markup, tests or images with no fetch, redirect, HTML sink, storage or analytics change, answer "no security-relevant change" in one line. Do not open files for that.
6. Read the changed files in full and grep callers before claiming a vulnerability; do not review from diff hunks alone.
7. If nothing exploitable is found, say "No exploitable issues found" and list what you checked.
